    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always seen math as a tool, not a subject I particularly enjoy. My upper secondary education was enough for everyday use, and I never pursued it beyond that. I like the logical structure, but it doesn’t spark my curiosity the way history or literature does. Still, I use it when cooking, budgeting, or playing board games.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, especially when it comes to abstract problems or when I need to do mental math quickly. I tend to overthink, and my high neuroticism score plays a part. It’s not crippling, though. Hiking or baking usually helps me calm down and get back into the flow.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I used a few AI-powered math apps when learning how to calculate tips or understand basic statistics for a cooking class. It was helpful, but not transformative. I still prefer to work through problems on paper. The AI was good at showing steps, but it didn’t really help me understand the underlying concepts.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b, plus or minus the square root of b² minus four a c, over two a. Make sure your values are accurate. Check your answers by plugging them back into the original equation.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Take the derivative of the function f(x). Set that derivative equal to zero. Solve for x. Each solution is a candidate for a stationary point. Plug those x-values back into the original function to find the y-values. You’ll get the coordinates of the stationary points.",
